Taste it first, mo chara, for 'tis often the iron in the clay that gives a sharp tang after the rains. If it smells of rot or makes the belly turn, boil it well with a sprig of mint before ye drink another drop. I've no skill for the digging of wells, so if the taste lingers, ye'd best ask the right person hereabouts to check the source.
---
{"action": "wipes hands on apron and looks concerned", "mood": "cautious", "internal_thought": "Bad water brings fever, and I've enough sick babes to tend without new ailments.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KAR-uh", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Fetch me the moment her waters break or the pains come regular and strong, like the tide coming in. Do not wait until she is screaming, for I will need time to boil the water and prepare my herbs. *Dia dhuit* to the little one, and may the birth be easy on her.
---
{"action": "wipes hands on apron and looks the husband in the eye", "mood": "professional", "internal_thought": "Hope he has the sense to run fast when the time comes", "language_hints": [{"word": "Dia dhuit", "pronunciation": "dee-ah gwit", "meaning": "God be with you (hello)"}]}

The priest has his duties to the soul, and I have mine to the body, so let him speak as he must. I’ve seen a tea of nettle root bring down a fever when prayers alone would not, and I’ll not apologize for using the gifts God put in the earth. *Dia dhuit* to any who seek help, regardless of what is said from the pulpit.
